WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.320 --> 00:00:04.960
when i build serverless systems i like

00:00:02.560 --> 00:00:07.359
to use a serverless nosql database like

00:00:04.960 --> 00:00:09.120
firestore or datastore i don't have to

00:00:07.359 --> 00:00:10.480
worry about maintenance and they scale

00:00:09.120 --> 00:00:12.000
up really well

00:00:10.480 --> 00:00:13.920
they do that because they're optimized

00:00:12.000 --> 00:00:15.920
for accessing only a few records at a

00:00:13.920 --> 00:00:18.560
time and that's fine for most web and

00:00:15.920 --> 00:00:21.199
mobile apps but to run a business you

00:00:18.560 --> 00:00:23.199
also need analytics and to do analytics

00:00:21.199 --> 00:00:25.279
you need to read thousands or millions

00:00:23.199 --> 00:00:28.240
of records at a time that's often too

00:00:25.279 --> 00:00:29.920
slow in nosql databases so how do we get

00:00:28.240 --> 00:00:30.880
the analytics that we need to run our

00:00:29.920 --> 00:00:32.480
business

00:00:30.880 --> 00:00:34.719
here's how i do it

00:00:32.480 --> 00:00:36.960
i run a nightly job that imports my

00:00:34.719 --> 00:00:38.640
nosql database into bigquery which is

00:00:36.960 --> 00:00:40.640
also serverless by the way

00:00:38.640 --> 00:00:42.960
bigquery is optimized for analytics and

00:00:40.640 --> 00:00:46.000
can easily run queries across millions

00:00:42.960 --> 00:00:48.320
of records also with separate databases

00:00:46.000 --> 00:00:49.760
the app can't slow down the analytics and

00:00:48.320 --> 00:00:51.600
vice versa

00:00:49.760 --> 00:00:53.280
now that the data is in bigquery it's

00:00:51.600 --> 00:00:55.760
easy to create a dashboard in data

00:00:53.280 --> 00:00:58.000
studio to drive business decisions

00:00:55.760 --> 00:01:01.440
how do you handle analytics for your app

00:00:58.000 --> 00:01:01.440
let me know in the comments

